hello we start with breaking news from  sudan where the sovereign council says  there's been a failed coup attempt the  plotters reportedly attempted to take  control of the state radio facility for  more on this heber morgan joins us on  the phone from khartoum hello there hiba  what more do we know about this failed  coup attempt  well there are still a lot of questions  about which group exactly is behind this  and the purpose of the coup what we do  know is that about three hours ago  khartoum walk up to what seemed like a  pretty normal morning with the exception  of that the one of the bridges leading  to uman which is one of which is the  twin city of the capital khartoum one of  those bridges were blocked and that  bridge leads to the arms temple of the  military now there were a couple of  tanks standing in front of the bridges  preventing civilians from crossing over  and there were a lot of ships by those  people who were trying to cross the  bridge as to why there were tanks uh  between those two bridges then came the  report that there was a coup attempt and  that the soldiers were taking over the  arms depot and that they wanted to take  over the state television and radio now  who exactly that group is and why they  try to stage a coup that's the question  we're waiting for uh to hear answers  from the sovereignty council they're  expecting to issue where they're  expected to issue a statement  uh in a short while um but so far we've  seen a lot of tweets from members of the  sovereignty council one of them being  the spokesman of the sovereignty council  himself who has called  on the people to support the military  and the transition and  other members of the government or  rather the ruling coalition of this  government are calling for support of  the transition of course this is a  government that came to power following  the overthrow of former president ahmad  bashir in april 2019 before uh signing a  power sharing agreement between the  military and the civilians so right now  there's a lot of questions on the minds  of many people here in sudan which we're  waiting to hear answers for uh  from the uh sovereignty council when  they issue a statement shortly  but give us an insight into you did  mention it briefly just then give us an  insight into the current political  situation internally and externally in  sudan  well there is a bit of tension both  sides have admitted admitted the  military and the civilians which are in  a power sharing agreement both admitted  that there are some tensions between the  two sides but nevertheless they've  always described their relationship as  something of a unique model that many  countries  can follow now uh there has been reports  of tensions between the rabbit support  forces that's the para group uh here in  sudan which is uh uh the head of that  group is also part of the sovereignty  council in fact he's the deputy head of  the sovereignty council uh there's been  rumors of tensions between his um his  forces and the military uh that that has  risen uh has caused tensions in the  capital repeatedly and then there's of  course  the the the relationship between the  military and the power sharing uh  coalition the coalition that they signed  a deal with uh in august of 2019.  there's been repeatedly war of wars  between the two sides we've seen  repeated uh statements issued by the two  sides condemning each other for moves  taken but nevertheless all sides have  repeated that they  have a unique kind of partnership a  unique structure that they're trying to  follow to ensure that sudan um actually  heads to elections and follows its path  on a democratic transition now sudan's  relationship with its neighbors has been  up and down there's of course egypt  which it had military drills with  several times over the past year but  then there's tensions between sudan and  ethiopia over the grand eastern  renaissance as well as a 3 000 square  kilometer area known as  which straddles the border between sudan  and ethiopia now again we're waiting for  statements from the army to understand  who exactly was behind the coup and the  purpose of the coup and exactly what  motivated them there's also another  thing that we have to take into account  now in the east of the country  in the state of red the state  specifically port sudan there has been a  lot of tension in fact the road leading  to that state has been blocked by the  tribe known as the bija who have  condemned the peace agreement  that was signed in the south sudanese  capital cuba last year they say that  even though there were members from the  east to sign on that peace agreement it  does not represent the demand so  internally that has been causing a lot  of tension to this government  heber how much does the economic  situation in the country come into play  here  now when this government took power  in august 2019 there were a lot of  protests that followed because of the  economic conditions the inflation was  continuing uh continuing to rise deeply  many people have complained that they  were no longer able to afford you know  basic commodities and we've seen the  rate of poverty go from 50  to over 70 percent that's something that  the government has admitted to but we've  also seen uh earlier this year the  government devaluing its currency and  floating its exchange rates to be able  to stabilize the economy according to  their words they've lifted fuel  subsidies and that has been something  that many of the official of the people  in the country have not accepted in the  stage protest uh against that but in the  past month there has been a report that  the inflation has actually gone down  and a lot of people are saying that  some to a certain degree the economic  situation is starting to stabilize so  for the past two or three months we have  not seen a lot of protests regarding the  economy talking to average people you  would hear people uh talking about the  economy saying that they still find  basic commodities unaffordable but a lot  of them are saying that they're starting  to see some changes in the economy  they're starting to see some  improvements especially since the  devaluation of the currency  heber tell us a little bit about the  mood on the street you're obviously very  familiar with sudan have you been able  to speak to anyone about their reactions  to this potential failed coup  well we've spoken to military sources to  get an understanding of what has  happened and they have said that this is  a small group and that they are  containing the situation now the work we  focus on here is containing which means  that it is still very much an active  operation that they're trying to control  then there's also uh the normal people  people who are trying to understand what  is happening  there has been a bit of traffic on the  street the uh you could see you could  send some kind of tension you know uh  that there is a bit of a difference from  everyday and this morning uh people  trying to understand what is happening a  lot of them waiting for that army  statement to let them know what has been  going under what has been happening and  what is still underway uh in the capital  hotsome and whether there is any danger  to the transition now people have been  repeatedly saying that they have always  felt that the military had an upper hand  uh in in the in the  controlling things in sudan many people  who protested let's remember said that  they did not want a military rule they  wanted a civilian rule uh as the current  things stand there is the prime minister  abdullah who is  the head of the executive council of the  transitional government then you have  the sovereignty council now to get an  understanding the sovereignty council is  made up of five military members six  civilian members from the ruling  coalition and then three members from  the armed groups that signed a peace  deal with the syrian government in  october and the southern his capital  cuba last year that's the makeup of the  transitional council and the executive  council which together at the moment  also found the legislative assembly  until a transitional legislative  assembly is elected by the ruling  coalition now a lot of people and a lot  of political parties have been saying  that they're not happy with the  situation  they believe that the military is in  control of many of the issues and now  that they are also armed groups in the  deal and in the government they believe  that the civilians are getting less and  less control nevertheless they've always  spoken about the fact that they're  waiting for this transitional government  to lead them to elections they wanted to  see uh syndicates and unions being  formed they wanted to see the the  building blocks and the foundations  being put for an election to be held by  the end of the transition so for a lot  of people who we've been speaking to  this morning there are questions as to  whether that transition is now in doubt  and whether the army will get a stronger  grip by the end of the day  what is your thoughts or what are your  thoughts on the strength of that  military you have alluded to it in that  previous answer but how strong are they  you're talking about an institution that  has been in power for over 60 years or  rather that has been in existence for  over 60 years  facing a coalition that has been in  power for less than two years now  obviously when you compare the two the  military has the upper mind they also  control a lot of companies and thus uh  can balance themselves with the sydney  economy and the ways things are managed  so a lot of people have been saying that  uh the army should let go of the  companies and the industries that they  control because that gives them a lot of  income that they should not be getting  and that they should be  on the payroll of the government just  like any other civil servants uh  obviously the guy that the army has a  lot of power especially when you factor  into the fact that it isn't some kind of  a partnership although tense with the  rapid support forces now that's the para  military group that has been leading the  uh the conflict in darfur and has been  accused of human human rights atrocities  including burning villages and raping  women so when you look at those two  groups together and then you look at the  civilian coalition which has been in  power for about two years it's obvious  to see who has the upper hand here but  then when you look at the document the  the the power sharing agreement that was  signed  that document gives people a bit of an  upper hand  there are a lot of talks about you know  how strong the executive council is how  strong is the prime minister when uh  when he's up against the military but  again the prime minister has always been  saying he's very careful not to rock the  boat as a matter of fact he has proposed  um he's made proposals to try to make  sure that this country does or rather  the transition continues and that the  country as for his words does not break  apart and that includes reforms in the  military so there has been more and more  tensions between the civilian side and  the military side and again when you  look at the institution that has uh  companies on uh under its control and  which it makes income off and an  institution that has been in existence  for over 60 years it's clear to see who  has the upper hand here  but thank you very much for your  insights there hibern morgan live for us  in khartoum no doubt we will cross back  to you as this story develops and we are  waiting to hear from the army and we'll  bring that to you live when we can thank  you hibba

